window.docContents[223].resolve({"/part-5-applications/ch17-ramsey/#ch17-ramsey":{"contents":"Chapter 17 (Ch17_RamseyTheory.lean, 2,064 lines) showcases how ultrafilters and nonstandard transfer streamline Ramsey combinatorics:\n\n1. Infinite Ramsey Theorem: Given a k-coloring c : [\\mathbb{N}]^r \\to \\{0, \\dots, k-1\\} of r-element subsets of \\mathbb{N}, evaluate {}^*c at an unlimited point \\omega \\in \\mathbb{N}^*_\\infty to induce a k-coloring c'(s) := {}^*c(s \\cup \\{\\omega\\}) on (r-1)-subsets! Inducting on r and stepping through standard witnesses via Transfer constructs an infinite monochromatic subset H \\subseteq \\mathbb{N} cleanly.2. Compactness Transfer to Finite Ramsey: Suppose for contradiction that for every n \\in \\mathbb{N} there existed a “bad” k-coloring c_n of \\{1, \\dots, n\\} with no monochromatic m-subset. In the ultrapower, the sequence (c_n) assembles into an internal coloring on \\{1, \\dots, N\\} (N unlimited), whose restriction to \\mathbb{N} violates Infinite Ramsey!3. Paris–Harrington Theorem (1977): Replacing fixed size |H| \\ge m with relatively large finite sets (|H| \\ge m and |H| \\ge \\min(H)) follows in a dozen lines from Infinite Ramsey by the exact same compactness-transfer argument—remarkably proving in ZFC a combinatorial principle famously independent of first-order Peano Arithmetic (PA)!\n\nsection\nopen Goldblatt.Ch17\n\n#check @ramsey_three_three_six\n#check @infinite_ramsey\n#check @infinite_poset_chain_or_antichain_via_ramsey\n#check @finite_ramsey\n#check @paris_harrington_ramsey\nend\n\n\n","context":"Lectures on the Hyperreals — Interactive Lean 4 & Spatial Edition\u0009Part V — Landmark Applications & Reading Guide","header":"5.2. Chapter 17: Ramsey Theory & the Paris–Harrington Theorem","id":"/part-5-applications/ch17-ramsey/#ch17-ramsey"}});